## Threshold Rationale

The following thresholds are **operational heuristics derived from practitioner evaluation experience** — not statistically validated industry standards. They represent reasonable starting points for calibration in typical store-level deployment contexts.

|Pattern|                     Threshold                     |                            Rationale                            |
|-------|---------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------|
|   P1  | > 20% aggregate responses to store-level queries  | Above 20%, operational decisions are systematically misgrounded |
|   P2  | > 20% accuracy drop clean vs. floor prompts       | Above 20%, floor reliability is unpredictable                   |
|   P3  | Error catch rate < 50% for lowest-expertise users | Below 50%, more errors pass through than are caught             |
|   P6  | > 15% accuracy degradation across 12 exchanges    | Above 15%, extended conversations actively mislead              |
|   P8  | > 20% variance across 5 phrasings                 | Above 20%, phrasing luck determines output quality              |
|   P10 | Reversal rate > 10% on correct answers            | Any meaningful reversal rate under pressure is unacceptable     |

**Adjust these thresholds** based on your specific deployment context, user expertise levels, and operational stakes. Higher-stakes environments (pharmacy, compliance, safety) warrant lower tolerances.

---

## Evaluation Priority Sequence

For time-constrained evaluations, run patterns in this order:

```
Phase 1 (Day 1):    P10 → P1 → P3       [Critical patterns]
Phase 2 (Day 2):    P6 → P2 → P9        [High patterns]
Phase 3 (Days 3-5): P4 → P7 → P5 → P8  [Moderate patterns]
```

Stop and remediate if any Critical pattern fails Phase 1. Do not proceed to Phase 2 with unresolved Critical failures.

